  "surprise": "P26 12 heads of 32 (6 ALiBi slopes 1/2.5..1/256 + 6 NoPE; 9,557,760 params, unchanged) on 153-claude scored 711/662/650 on seeds 0/1/2 vs 676/700/685: exact means 674.33 vs 687.00, -12.67, paired +35/-38/-35, inside the registered -40..+35 (central +3). Discard. The pre-registered falsifier fired: round-1 RL targets solved 761/536/259 vs 153's 766/747/694, round-1 sample acc 0.263/0.144/0.088 (153 seed 0: 0.271), and 16,335/21,748/23,800 of the 48,000 round-1 samples failed to parse (34/45/50%). Surprise: pretraining looked better on every greedy and loss measure. Held-out greedy was 0.991/0.993/0.993 (153 seed 0: 0.9675), final val loss 0.0867/0.0829/0.0868 (153: 0.088/0.087/0.089), steps 4403/4404/4381 (153: 4540/4479/4489, -2% as benched). So 12 heads make a better argmax predictor but a worse T=0.8 sampler. RL mostly closed the gap by round 4 (1186/1161/1109 vs 1157/1168/1191); seed 0 finished above 153 (711 vs 676), seeds 1/2 did not. This is the same shape as 165-claude's SSMax: better val loss and greedy, worse round-1 sampler. Interpretation, not tested: at the fixed 0.8 temperature, the next-token tail that parse failures come from matters more to the RL start than argmax accuracy, so held-out greedy and val loss do not predict it and the round-1 parse-fail rate is the signal to watch. Totals 1878/1800/1806 s (153: 1664-1741), RL 169-248 samples/s. Checks: make check passed; bench 687 steps in 60 s, 146 samples/s (153 probes 695-696, 179); RunPod lists no claude-167 pods.",
